<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lough Erne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lough Erne is a pair of connected lakes on the River Erne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-fermanagh">County Fermanagh</a>, Northern Ireland. Ptolemy's Erdini are usually placed around it. In the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its islands and shores formed the heart of the Maguire lordship of Fermanagh, with monasteries such as Devenish and the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enniskillen">Enniskillen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lough-erne.jpg" alt="Lough Erne" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sam2453 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loch_Erne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lough Erne is the second-largest lake system in Northern Ireland and Ulster and the fourth largest in Ireland. The two lakes are widened sections of the River Erne, which is 80 miles long and drains about 1,680 square miles before it flows north and curves west into the Atlantic. The smaller southern lake is the Upper Lough, and the larger northern lake is the Lower Lough or Broad Lough.</p><p>The town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enniskillen">Enniskillen</a> stands on the short stretch of river between them. The lakes have more than 150 islands, many of which were islands in the strict sense before drainage schemes in the 1880s and the 1950s lowered the water by about 1.5 metres. The largest are Inishmore, Galloon Island and Boa Island. A gazetteer of 1846 estimated 90 islands in the Upper Lake and 109 in the Lower.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Loch Éirne appears to come from an ancient people, the Érainn, or from a goddess from whom they took their name. Irish tradition gives three accounts of the lake's origin: that it formed from the drowned body of Erne, lady-in-waiting to Queen Méabh; that a magic spring overflowed; or that it burst from the ground and drowned the Érainn in a battle with the army of High King Fíachu Labrainne.</p><p>It was also called Loch Saimer. The Menapii, the only Celtic tribe named on Ptolemy's map of Ireland, later settled around the lake and became known as the Fir Manach, who gave their name to Fermanagh. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> were written in the late fifteenth century on Belle Isle, an island in the Upper Lough.</p><p>In the Second World War RAF Castle Archdale on the lough was an airbase for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-atlantic">Battle of the Atlantic</a>. A secret agreement with the Irish government let flying boats fly west across neutral Ireland to the Atlantic along what was called the Donegal Corridor, avoiding a detour of two hours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Waterways Ireland, created under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/good-friday-agreement">Good Friday Agreement</a> in 1999, is responsible for navigation on the Erne system. Water levels have been managed since 1950, when the river was harnessed for hydroelectric power at Cliff, under an agreement with the Electricity Supply Board of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-ireland">Republic of Ireland</a>. The lough is popular for angling, sailing and watersports, and the Lough Erne Regatta is Ireland's oldest event for racing under sail. Visitors come to the monastic ruins on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devenish-island">Devenish Island</a> and the stone figures on Boa Island and White Island. The Shannon–Erne Waterway links the lake system to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-shannon">River Shannon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lough Erne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Erdini are named in Ptolemy's Geography of about 150, placed inland in the north-west of Ireland, usually located around <b>Lough Erne</b> in modern Fermanagh and neighbouring parts of Donegal.</blockquote><a href="/state/erdini">Erdini</a></li><li><blockquote>Fermanagh was organised around <b>Lough Erne</b>, with islands, crannogs and monasteries such as Devenish.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-fermanagh">Kingdom of Fermanagh</a></li></ul></section>
